        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
          A 2019 Maruti Swift VXI with 42,000 km on the odometer. One website says ₹4.2 lakh. Another says ₹4.8 lakh. A dealer in Navallur, Chennai, quotes ₹5.1 lakh over the phone without even seeing the car. Three numbers, three sources, and three completely different implications for a seller trying to decide whether to accept, negotiate, or walk away.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Used car valuation is the process of estimating the current market value of a pre-owned car</strong> based on its make, model, variant, age, kilometres driven, condition, ownership history, and the city where it's being sold. In India, used car valuations can be obtained free online through tools like TheResaleValue's <a href="/valuation">car value calculator</a>, which provides a fair-price band based on transaction data from over 10 million sales across Indian cities. That phrase "fair-price band" matters more than most sellers realise. A single number creates false precision. A range reflects how cars actually trade.
        </p>

        <p>
          The gap between those three Swift valuations isn't a bug. It's how the used car market actually functions. Each platform pulls from different data. One relies on listed prices from classifieds. Another draws on dealer buy-side offers. The third might factor in auction results. Their depreciation models differ, their intent differs (a buy-side platform wants to undervalue; a sell-side platform wants to attract listings), and their geographic weighting differs. Grasping this is the first step toward not getting the price wrong.
        </p>

        <p>
          This page anchors everything TheResaleValue publishes about used car pricing in India, from <a href="/new-car-depreciation">how used car depreciation works year by year</a> to city-level trends and route-to-sale comparisons. The sections below unpack how the calculator works, what actually drives value up or down, and when to ignore the online number entirely.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">How the car value calculator works, inputs, data layer, and what the output actually tells you</h2>

        <p>
          Most online car valuation tools look similar on the surface. Enter details, get a number. But the quality of that number depends on two things: what goes in and what data sits behind the calculation. Skip either layer and the result feels precise but misleads.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">What you enter (and why registration number alone isn't enough)</h3>

        <p>
          The inputs that shape a meaningful valuation: make, model, variant, year of manufacture, kilometres driven, city of registration or sale, and ownership count (first, second, third). Some tools also ask about insurance status and service history, which tighten the estimate.
        </p>

        <p>
          Registration-number-only tools have a specific limitation. They pull RTO data, which gives make, model, year, fuel type, and owner count. Useful but incomplete. The RTO database doesn't record kilometres driven. It doesn't distinguish between a base VXI and a top-spec ZXI+ variant, and that distinction can mean a ₹1.5–2.5 lakh difference in resale for popular models. It also doesn't capture whether the car has been sitting in a garage for two years or driven daily on Bangalore's Outer Ring Road.
        </p>

        <p>
          For the Bangalore market specifically, variant-level granularity matters even more because the mix of automatic and manual transmissions sold there skews heavily toward automatics in IT corridors. The resale premium for automatics in Bangalore is measurably higher than in, say, Jaipur or Bhopal.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The transaction data behind the number (listed price ≠ sold price)</h3>

        <p>
          Here's what separates a useful valuation from a misleading one. Most competitors build their models on listed prices, the asking price a seller or dealer posts on a classified platform. Listed prices are aspirational. The actual transaction price, what the car eventually sells for after negotiation, is typically 8–15% lower.
        </p>

        <p>
          That gap isn't uniform across cities. In Mumbai, where buyers are more negotiation-savvy and have more options, the discount from listed to sold tends to be wider for common models. In smaller cities with fewer listings and less buyer competition, listed prices and sold prices sit closer together.
        </p>

        <p>
          Read more about <a href="/methodology">our valuation methodology</a> for the full technical breakdown of how the data layer is constructed and updated.
        </p>

